---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> A2: Briefly describe what happens in a call to timer_sleep(),
>> including the effects of the timer interrupt handler.

timer_sleep() sleeps the active thread for 'ticks' ticks by
calling thread_sleep() with the current tick + the amount of ticks to sleep

thread_sleep() sets the threads wakeup-tick field, then inserts the thread into 
the sleep_list ordered by its wakeup-tick via list_insert_ordered with 
wakeup_tick_lessas the comparator function, then it blocks the thread by 
calling thread_block()

wakeup_tick_less() compares two threads a and b, and 
returns true if a's wakeup-tick field value < b's wakeup-tick field value

timer_interrupt() increments ticks, calls thread_tick() 
and the calls wakeup_threads()

wakeup_threads() continuosly compares the current tick against the thread with 
the smallest wakeup-tick field in the sleep_list. If the 
current tick > wakeup-tick of the earliest thread (the foremost item in the 
list), then the function returns with no effect. 
Otherwise it will continue to wake up threads by calling list_pop_front()
and calling thread_unblock on the popped thread



>> A3: What steps are taken to minimize the amount of time spent in
>> the timer interrupt handler?
The sleeping threads are ordered by increasing wakeup-tick so the
earliest thread to be woken up is at the front of the list. 
Thus scanning the list to see if a wakeup needs to be perfomed 
is only o(1) by peeking at the front of the linked list. In an 
unsorted list the check would need to be o(n) as we would
scan the entire sleep_list

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> A4: How are race conditions avoided when multiple threads call
>> timer_sleep() simultaneously?

timer_sleep() calls thread_sleep() in which interrupts 
are disabled (intr_disable()) before any writing is done 
to wakeup-ticks field and any threads are blocked and 
moved to the sleep_list. No race conditions as these 
two operations will be atomic.

>> A5: How are race conditions avoided when a timer interrupt occurs
>> during a call to timer_sleep()?
If an interrupt happens before a thread is 
slept, an interrupt could cause start to be 
slightly stale. Once inside thread_sleep() 
interrupts are disabled before wakeup_tick
and sleep_list are modified so a timer interrupt will
not corrupt any ordering or result in a partially 
modified thread. 

---- RATIONALE ----

>> A6: Why did you choose this design?  In what ways is it superior to
>> another design you considered?
1. ordered vs unordered sleep_list: by ordering by 
wakeup_tick we ensure that checking if any wake-ups
need to happen to have time complexity O(1) vs O(n) 
(checking the front of the list vs entire list). 
However this results in insertions being O(n) vs O(1). 
However, we still chose this approach because checking 
for wake up happens on every timer interrupt which is magnitudes more frequent 
than insertion into the list, so overall it is more effecient.

2. sleep_list vs busy_waiting. The original implementation just spun 
in a while loop calling thread_yield(). The new design blocks
the thread and only wakes it up when it is time. This is superior 
because now blocked threads dont consume CPU time and cause unneeded 
context switches. 

